---
title: TanStack Pacer Angular Adapter
id: adapter
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you are using TanStack Pacer in an Angular application, we recommend using the Angular Adapter. The Angular Adapter provides inject functions that wrap the core Pacer utilities and integrate with Angular's dependency injection and signals. If you need to use the core Pacer classes or functions directly, the Angular Adapter also re-exports everything from the core package."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"installation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Installation"}]},{"type":"code","value":"npm install @tanstack/angular-pacer","lang":"sh"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"angular-inject-api","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Angular inject API"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./reference/index.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Angular inject API Reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full list of inject functions (injectDebouncer, injectThrottler, injectRateLimiter, injectQueuer, injectBatcher, and their async and callback variants)."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"basic-usage","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic usage"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inject a Pacer utility in your component or service. Each inject function returns an object that exposes methods and a reactive "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state()"},{"type":"text","value":" signal when you pass a selector."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core'\nimport { injectDebouncer } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-root',\n  template: `\n    <input [value]=\"query()\" (input)=\"onInput($event)\" placeholder=\"Search...\" />\n    <p>Pending: {{ debouncer.state().isPending }}</p>\n    <p>Debounced: {{ debounced() }}</p>\n  `,\n})\nexport class App {\n  protected readonly query = signal('')\n  protected readonly debounced = signal('')\n\n  protected readonly debouncer = injectDebouncer(\n    (q: string) => {\n      this.debounced.set(q)\n    },\n    { wait: 500 },\n    (state) => ({ isPending: state.isPending }),\n  )\n\n  protected onInput(event: Event): void {\n    const value = (event.target as HTMLInputElement).value\n    this.query.set(value)\n    this.debouncer.maybeExecute(value)\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also import core Pacer APIs re-exported from the adapter."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { debounce, Debouncer } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"provider","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provider"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"providePacerOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" in your application config to set default options for all Pacer utilities in the app. Options passed to individual inject functions override these defaults."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { ApplicationConfig } from '@angular/core'\nimport { providePacerOptions } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\nexport const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {\n  providers: [\n    providePacerOptions({\n      debouncer: { wait: 300 },\n      throttler: { wait: 100 },\n      asyncQueuer: { concurrency: 2 },\n      rateLimiter: { limit: 5, window: 60000 },\n    }),\n  ],\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"state-selector","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State selector"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The third argument to each inject function is a state selector. It determines which slice of state is exposed on the returned object's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state()"},{"type":"text","value":" signal, so only relevant changes trigger template updates."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, if you omit the selector, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state()"},{"type":"text","value":" is not populated."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Pass a selector to opt in to reactive state."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// No selector: state() is not populated\nconst debouncer = injectDebouncer(fn, { wait: 500 })\n\n// With selector: state() is a signal of the selected slice\nconst debouncer = injectDebouncer(\n  fn,\n  { wait: 500 },\n  (state) => ({ isPending: state.isPending }),\n)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For more on state and options per utility, see the guides (e.g. "},{"type":"link","href":"./guides/debouncing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Debouncing Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"./guides/rate-limiting.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rate Limiting Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":")."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"examples","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"debouncer","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Debouncer"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core'\nimport { injectDebouncer } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-search',\n  template: `\n    <input [value]=\"query()\" (input)=\"onInput($event)\" placeholder=\"Search...\" />\n  `,\n})\nexport class SearchComponent {\n  protected readonly query = signal('')\n\n  protected readonly debouncer = injectDebouncer(\n    (q: string) => console.log('Searching for', q),\n    { wait: 500 },\n  )\n\n  protected onInput(event: Event): void {\n    const value = (event.target as HTMLInputElement).value\n    this.query.set(value)\n    this.debouncer.maybeExecute(value)\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"async-queuer","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async Queuer"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core'\nimport { injectAsyncQueuer } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-upload',\n  template: `\n    <input type=\"file\" multiple (change)=\"onFiles($event)\" />\n    <p>Queue size: {{ queuer.state().size }}</p>\n  `,\n})\nexport class UploadComponent {\n  protected readonly queuer = injectAsyncQueuer<File, { size: number }>(\n    async (file) => {\n      await uploadFile(file)\n    },\n    { concurrency: 3 },\n    (state) => ({ size: state.size }),\n  )\n\n  protected onFiles(event: Event): void {\n    const files = (event.target as HTMLInputElement).files\n    if (files) {\n      Array.from(files).forEach((file) => this.queuer.addItem(file))\n    }\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"rate-limiter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rate Limiter"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { Component } from '@angular/core'\nimport { injectRateLimiter } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-api',\n  template: `\n    <button (click)=\"submit()\">Submit</button>\n    <p>Rejections: {{ rateLimiter.state().rejectionCount }}</p>\n  `,\n})\nexport class ApiComponent {\n  protected readonly rateLimiter = injectRateLimiter<string, { rejectionCount: number }>(\n    (data) =>\n      fetch('/api/endpoint', {\n        method: 'POST',\n        body: JSON.stringify({ data }),\n      }),\n    {\n      limit: 5,\n      window: 60000,\n      onReject: () => alert('Rate limit reached. Try again later.'),\n    },\n    (state) => ({ rejectionCount: state.rejectionCount }),\n  )\n\n  protected submit(): void {\n    this.rateLimiter.maybeExecute('payload')\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reactive-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reactive options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use property getters or an options factory to read Angular signals:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const wait = signal(300)\nconst debouncer = injectDebouncer(save, {\n  get wait() {\n    return wait()\n  },\n})\n\nwait.set(600)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Factories also work with required component inputs:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { input } from '@angular/core'\nimport { injectDebouncer } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\nreadonly wait = input.required<number>()\nreadonly search = injectDebouncer(\n  (query: string) => this.fetchResults(query),\n  () => ({ wait: this.wait() }),\n)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Getter-based options and factories defer initialization until Angular's first effect, after component inputs are bound, or until you first access the returned utility. Accessing the utility before a required input is available throws Angular's required-input error. Destroying the component before initialization does not read those options or create a utility. Plain objects without getters initialize eagerly when their provider defaults also contain no getters."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Later signal changes update the same utility through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" during change detection. Provider defaults are read during option tracking, and local options override them. This contract applies to synchronous and asynchronous inject functions and their callback, signal, and value helpers."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reading a signal before passing the options, such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ wait: wait() }"},{"type":"text","value":", produces a snapshot. Assigning to an ordinary object property does not trigger an update. Use a getter or factory for reactive values. Option reads are shallow: build nested configurations inside a getter or factory when they depend on signals. Callbacks and function-valued core options remain functions. Getters and factories should read signals and return options without side effects."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Updates preserve the utility, store, queued items, and pending work. Changing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wait"},{"type":"text","value":" does not reschedule an existing timer. Setting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" still applies the utility's normal cancellation behavior. Construction options such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialItems"},{"type":"text","value":" apply only when the utility is created. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start()"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stop()"},{"type":"text","value":" to change running queues."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Updates follow "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" merge semantics. If a factory omits a previously supplied field, the provider default replaces it when one exists; otherwise, its previous value remains. Return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" explicitly to clear an optional field. For example, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUnmount: undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" restores default cleanup. Disposal uses the latest "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUnmount"},{"type":"text","value":" callback. The utility's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"options"},{"type":"text","value":" property exposes its current core options, including manual "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" updates."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a value helper with an explicit initial value and factory options, pass a fourth argument for the selector. Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" when no selector is needed:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const debounced = injectDebouncedValue(\n  query,\n  '',\n  () => ({ wait: wait() }),\n  undefined,\n)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The fourth argument distinguishes this form from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"injectDebouncedValue(query, optionsFactory, selector)"},{"type":"text","value":". Object options still support the existing three-argument form with an initial value."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"event-handlers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Event handlers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"injectDebouncer"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"injectThrottler"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"injectRateLimiter"},{"type":"text","value":" and call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maybeExecute()"},{"type":"text","value":" from the event handler. For batching, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"injectBatcher"},{"type":"text","value":" and call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addItem()"},{"type":"text","value":". Async utilities use the same method names."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Store the utility instance in a field when options read required inputs. Accessing a method during field initialization can initialize the utility before Angular binds those inputs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In a component:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"readonly wait = input.required<number>()\nreadonly debouncer = injectDebouncer(saveDraft, () => ({ wait: this.wait() }))\n\nsave(draft: string) {\n  this.debouncer.maybeExecute(draft)\n}","lang":"ts"}],"headings":[{"id":"installation","text":"Installation","level":2},{"id":"angular-inject-api","text":"Angular inject API","level":2},{"id":"basic-usage","text":"Basic usage","level":2},{"id":"provider","text":"Provider","level":2},{"id":"state-selector","text":"State selector","level":2},{"id":"examples","text":"Examples","level":2},{"id":"debouncer","text":"Debouncer","level":3},{"id":"async-queuer","text":"Async Queuer","level":3},{"id":"rate-limiter","text":"Rate Limiter","level":3},{"id":"reactive-options","text":"Reactive options","level":2},{"id":"event-handlers","text":"Event handlers","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nIf you are using TanStack Pacer in an Angular application, we recommend using the Angular Adapter. The Angular Adapter provides inject functions that wrap the core Pacer utilities and integrate with Angular's dependency injection and signals. If you need to use the core Pacer classes or functions directly, the Angular Adapter also re-exports everything from the core package.\n\n## Installation\n\n```sh\nnpm install @tanstack/angular-pacer\n```\n\n## Angular inject API\n\nSee the [Angular inject API Reference](./reference/index.md) for the full list of inject functions (injectDebouncer, injectThrottler, injectRateLimiter, injectQueuer, injectBatcher, and their async and callback variants).\n\n## Basic usage\n\nInject a Pacer utility in your component or service. Each inject function returns an object that exposes methods and a reactive `state()` signal when you pass a selector.\n\n```ts\nimport { Component, signal } from '@angular/core'\nimport { injectDebouncer } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-root',\n  template: `\n    <input [value]=\"query()\" (input)=\"onInput($event)\" placeholder=\"Search...\" />\n    <p>Pending: {{ debouncer.state().isPending }}</p>\n    <p>Debounced: {{ debounced() }}</p>\n  `,\n})\nexport class App {\n  protected readonly query = signal('')\n  protected readonly debounced = signal('')\n\n  protected readonly debouncer = injectDebouncer(\n    (q: string) => {\n      this.debounced.set(q)\n    },\n    { wait: 500 },\n    (state) => ({ isPending: state.isPending }),\n  )\n\n  protected onInput(event: Event): void {\n    const value = (event.target as HTMLInputElement).value\n    this.query.set(value)\n    this.debouncer.maybeExecute(value)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nYou can also import core Pacer APIs re-exported from the adapter.\n\n```ts\nimport { debounce, Debouncer } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n```\n\n## Provider\n\nUse `providePacerOptions` in your application config to set default options for all Pacer utilities in the app. Options passed to individual inject functions override these defaults.\n\n```ts\nimport { ApplicationConfig } from '@angular/core'\nimport { providePacerOptions } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\nexport const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {\n  providers: [\n    providePacerOptions({\n      debouncer: { wait: 300 },\n      throttler: { wait: 100 },\n      asyncQueuer: { concurrency: 2 },\n      rateLimiter: { limit: 5, window: 60000 },\n    }),\n  ],\n}\n```\n\n## State selector\n\nThe third argument to each inject function is a state selector. It determines which slice of state is exposed on the returned object's `state()` signal, so only relevant changes trigger template updates.\n\n**By default, if you omit the selector, `state()` is not populated.** Pass a selector to opt in to reactive state.\n\n```ts\n// No selector: state() is not populated\nconst debouncer = injectDebouncer(fn, { wait: 500 })\n\n// With selector: state() is a signal of the selected slice\nconst debouncer = injectDebouncer(\n  fn,\n  { wait: 500 },\n  (state) => ({ isPending: state.isPending }),\n)\n```\n\nFor more on state and options per utility, see the guides (e.g. [Debouncing Guide](./guides/debouncing.md), [Rate Limiting Guide](./guides/rate-limiting.md)).\n\n## Examples\n\n### Debouncer\n\n```ts\nimport { Component, signal } from '@angular/core'\nimport { injectDebouncer } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-search',\n  template: `\n    <input [value]=\"query()\" (input)=\"onInput($event)\" placeholder=\"Search...\" />\n  `,\n})\nexport class SearchComponent {\n  protected readonly query = signal('')\n\n  protected readonly debouncer = injectDebouncer(\n    (q: string) => console.log('Searching for', q),\n    { wait: 500 },\n  )\n\n  protected onInput(event: Event): void {\n    const value = (event.target as HTMLInputElement).value\n    this.query.set(value)\n    this.debouncer.maybeExecute(value)\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Async Queuer\n\n```ts\nimport { Component, signal } from '@angular/core'\nimport { injectAsyncQueuer } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-upload',\n  template: `\n    <input type=\"file\" multiple (change)=\"onFiles($event)\" />\n    <p>Queue size: {{ queuer.state().size }}</p>\n  `,\n})\nexport class UploadComponent {\n  protected readonly queuer = injectAsyncQueuer<File, { size: number }>(\n    async (file) => {\n      await uploadFile(file)\n    },\n    { concurrency: 3 },\n    (state) => ({ size: state.size }),\n  )\n\n  protected onFiles(event: Event): void {\n    const files = (event.target as HTMLInputElement).files\n    if (files) {\n      Array.from(files).forEach((file) => this.queuer.addItem(file))\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Rate Limiter\n\n```ts\nimport { Component } from '@angular/core'\nimport { injectRateLimiter } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-api',\n  template: `\n    <button (click)=\"submit()\">Submit</button>\n    <p>Rejections: {{ rateLimiter.state().rejectionCount }}</p>\n  `,\n})\nexport class ApiComponent {\n  protected readonly rateLimiter = injectRateLimiter<string, { rejectionCount: number }>(\n    (data) =>\n      fetch('/api/endpoint', {\n        method: 'POST',\n        body: JSON.stringify({ data }),\n      }),\n    {\n      limit: 5,\n      window: 60000,\n      onReject: () => alert('Rate limit reached. Try again later.'),\n    },\n    (state) => ({ rejectionCount: state.rejectionCount }),\n  )\n\n  protected submit(): void {\n    this.rateLimiter.maybeExecute('payload')\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Reactive options\n\nUse property getters or an options factory to read Angular signals:\n\n```ts\nconst wait = signal(300)\nconst debouncer = injectDebouncer(save, {\n  get wait() {\n    return wait()\n  },\n})\n\nwait.set(600)\n```\n\nFactories also work with required component inputs:\n\n```ts\nimport { input } from '@angular/core'\nimport { injectDebouncer } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\nreadonly wait = input.required<number>()\nreadonly search = injectDebouncer(\n  (query: string) => this.fetchResults(query),\n  () => ({ wait: this.wait() }),\n)\n```\n\nGetter-based options and factories defer initialization until Angular's first effect, after component inputs are bound, or until you first access the returned utility. Accessing the utility before a required input is available throws Angular's required-input error. Destroying the component before initialization does not read those options or create a utility. Plain objects without getters initialize eagerly when their provider defaults also contain no getters.\n\nLater signal changes update the same utility through `setOptions` during change detection. Provider defaults are read during option tracking, and local options override them. This contract applies to synchronous and asynchronous inject functions and their callback, signal, and value helpers.\n\nReading a signal before passing the options, such as `{ wait: wait() }`, produces a snapshot. Assigning to an ordinary object property does not trigger an update. Use a getter or factory for reactive values. Option reads are shallow: build nested configurations inside a getter or factory when they depend on signals. Callbacks and function-valued core options remain functions. Getters and factories should read signals and return options without side effects.\n\nUpdates preserve the utility, store, queued items, and pending work. Changing `wait` does not reschedule an existing timer. Setting `enabled` to `false` still applies the utility's normal cancellation behavior. Construction options such as `key`, `initialState`, and `initialItems` apply only when the utility is created. Use `start()` and `stop()` to change running queues.\n\nUpdates follow `setOptions` merge semantics. If a factory omits a previously supplied field, the provider default replaces it when one exists; otherwise, its previous value remains. Return `undefined` explicitly to clear an optional field. For example, `onUnmount: undefined` restores default cleanup. Disposal uses the latest `onUnmount` callback. The utility's `options` property exposes its current core options, including manual `setOptions` updates.\n\nFor a value helper with an explicit initial value and factory options, pass a fourth argument for the selector. Pass `undefined` when no selector is needed:\n\n```ts\nconst debounced = injectDebouncedValue(\n  query,\n  '',\n  () => ({ wait: wait() }),\n  undefined,\n)\n```\n\nThe fourth argument distinguishes this form from `injectDebouncedValue(query, optionsFactory, selector)`. Object options still support the existing three-argument form with an initial value.\n\n## Event handlers\n\nUse `injectDebouncer`, `injectThrottler`, or `injectRateLimiter` and call `maybeExecute()` from the event handler. For batching, use `injectBatcher` and call `addItem()`. Async utilities use the same method names.\n\nStore the utility instance in a field when options read required inputs. Accessing a method during field initialization can initialize the utility before Angular binds those inputs.\n\nIn a component:\n\n```ts\nreadonly wait = input.required<number>()\nreadonly debouncer = injectDebouncer(saveDraft, () => ({ wait: this.wait() }))\n\nsave(draft: string) {\n  this.debouncer.maybeExecute(draft)\n}\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: TanStack Pacer Angular Adapter\nid: adapter\n---\n\nIf you are using TanStack Pacer in an Angular application, we recommend using the Angular Adapter. The Angular Adapter provides inject functions that wrap the core Pacer utilities and integrate with Angular's dependency injection and signals. If you need to use the core Pacer classes or functions directly, the Angular Adapter also re-exports everything from the core package.\n\n## Installation\n\n```sh\nnpm install @tanstack/angular-pacer\n```\n\n## Angular inject API\n\nSee the [Angular inject API Reference](./reference/index.md) for the full list of inject functions (injectDebouncer, injectThrottler, injectRateLimiter, injectQueuer, injectBatcher, and their async and callback variants).\n\n## Basic usage\n\nInject a Pacer utility in your component or service. Each inject function returns an object that exposes methods and a reactive `state()` signal when you pass a selector.\n\n```ts\nimport { Component, signal } from '@angular/core'\nimport { injectDebouncer } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-root',\n  template: `\n    <input [value]=\"query()\" (input)=\"onInput($event)\" placeholder=\"Search...\" />\n    <p>Pending: {{ debouncer.state().isPending }}</p>\n    <p>Debounced: {{ debounced() }}</p>\n  `,\n})\nexport class App {\n  protected readonly query = signal('')\n  protected readonly debounced = signal('')\n\n  protected readonly debouncer = injectDebouncer(\n    (q: string) => {\n      this.debounced.set(q)\n    },\n    { wait: 500 },\n    (state) => ({ isPending: state.isPending }),\n  )\n\n  protected onInput(event: Event): void {\n    const value = (event.target as HTMLInputElement).value\n    this.query.set(value)\n    this.debouncer.maybeExecute(value)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nYou can also import core Pacer APIs re-exported from the adapter.\n\n```ts\nimport { debounce, Debouncer } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n```\n\n## Provider\n\nUse `providePacerOptions` in your application config to set default options for all Pacer utilities in the app. Options passed to individual inject functions override these defaults.\n\n```ts\nimport { ApplicationConfig } from '@angular/core'\nimport { providePacerOptions } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\nexport const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {\n  providers: [\n    providePacerOptions({\n      debouncer: { wait: 300 },\n      throttler: { wait: 100 },\n      asyncQueuer: { concurrency: 2 },\n      rateLimiter: { limit: 5, window: 60000 },\n    }),\n  ],\n}\n```\n\n## State selector\n\nThe third argument to each inject function is a state selector. It determines which slice of state is exposed on the returned object's `state()` signal, so only relevant changes trigger template updates.\n\n**By default, if you omit the selector, `state()` is not populated.** Pass a selector to opt in to reactive state.\n\n```ts\n// No selector: state() is not populated\nconst debouncer = injectDebouncer(fn, { wait: 500 })\n\n// With selector: state() is a signal of the selected slice\nconst debouncer = injectDebouncer(\n  fn,\n  { wait: 500 },\n  (state) => ({ isPending: state.isPending }),\n)\n```\n\nFor more on state and options per utility, see the guides (e.g. [Debouncing Guide](./guides/debouncing.md), [Rate Limiting Guide](./guides/rate-limiting.md)).\n\n## Examples\n\n### Debouncer\n\n```ts\nimport { Component, signal } from '@angular/core'\nimport { injectDebouncer } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-search',\n  template: `\n    <input [value]=\"query()\" (input)=\"onInput($event)\" placeholder=\"Search...\" />\n  `,\n})\nexport class SearchComponent {\n  protected readonly query = signal('')\n\n  protected readonly debouncer = injectDebouncer(\n    (q: string) => console.log('Searching for', q),\n    { wait: 500 },\n  )\n\n  protected onInput(event: Event): void {\n    const value = (event.target as HTMLInputElement).value\n    this.query.set(value)\n    this.debouncer.maybeExecute(value)\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Async Queuer\n\n```ts\nimport { Component, signal } from '@angular/core'\nimport { injectAsyncQueuer } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-upload',\n  template: `\n    <input type=\"file\" multiple (change)=\"onFiles($event)\" />\n    <p>Queue size: {{ queuer.state().size }}</p>\n  `,\n})\nexport class UploadComponent {\n  protected readonly queuer = injectAsyncQueuer<File, { size: number }>(\n    async (file) => {\n      await uploadFile(file)\n    },\n    { concurrency: 3 },\n    (state) => ({ size: state.size }),\n  )\n\n  protected onFiles(event: Event): void {\n    const files = (event.target as HTMLInputElement).files\n    if (files) {\n      Array.from(files).forEach((file) => this.queuer.addItem(file))\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Rate Limiter\n\n```ts\nimport { Component } from '@angular/core'\nimport { injectRateLimiter } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-api',\n  template: `\n    <button (click)=\"submit()\">Submit</button>\n    <p>Rejections: {{ rateLimiter.state().rejectionCount }}</p>\n  `,\n})\nexport class ApiComponent {\n  protected readonly rateLimiter = injectRateLimiter<string, { rejectionCount: number }>(\n    (data) =>\n      fetch('/api/endpoint', {\n        method: 'POST',\n        body: JSON.stringify({ data }),\n      }),\n    {\n      limit: 5,\n      window: 60000,\n      onReject: () => alert('Rate limit reached. Try again later.'),\n    },\n    (state) => ({ rejectionCount: state.rejectionCount }),\n  )\n\n  protected submit(): void {\n    this.rateLimiter.maybeExecute('payload')\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Reactive options\n\nUse property getters or an options factory to read Angular signals:\n\n```ts\nconst wait = signal(300)\nconst debouncer = injectDebouncer(save, {\n  get wait() {\n    return wait()\n  },\n})\n\nwait.set(600)\n```\n\nFactories also work with required component inputs:\n\n```ts\nimport { input } from '@angular/core'\nimport { injectDebouncer } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\nreadonly wait = input.required<number>()\nreadonly search = injectDebouncer(\n  (query: string) => this.fetchResults(query),\n  () => ({ wait: this.wait() }),\n)\n```\n\nGetter-based options and factories defer initialization until Angular's first effect, after component inputs are bound, or until you first access the returned utility. Accessing the utility before a required input is available throws Angular's required-input error. Destroying the component before initialization does not read those options or create a utility. Plain objects without getters initialize eagerly when their provider defaults also contain no getters.\n\nLater signal changes update the same utility through `setOptions` during change detection. Provider defaults are read during option tracking, and local options override them. This contract applies to synchronous and asynchronous inject functions and their callback, signal, and value helpers.\n\nReading a signal before passing the options, such as `{ wait: wait() }`, produces a snapshot. Assigning to an ordinary object property does not trigger an update. Use a getter or factory for reactive values. Option reads are shallow: build nested configurations inside a getter or factory when they depend on signals. Callbacks and function-valued core options remain functions. Getters and factories should read signals and return options without side effects.\n\nUpdates preserve the utility, store, queued items, and pending work. Changing `wait` does not reschedule an existing timer. Setting `enabled` to `false` still applies the utility's normal cancellation behavior. Construction options such as `key`, `initialState`, and `initialItems` apply only when the utility is created. Use `start()` and `stop()` to change running queues.\n\nUpdates follow `setOptions` merge semantics. If a factory omits a previously supplied field, the provider default replaces it when one exists; otherwise, its previous value remains. Return `undefined` explicitly to clear an optional field. For example, `onUnmount: undefined` restores default cleanup. Disposal uses the latest `onUnmount` callback. The utility's `options` property exposes its current core options, including manual `setOptions` updates.\n\nFor a value helper with an explicit initial value and factory options, pass a fourth argument for the selector. Pass `undefined` when no selector is needed:\n\n```ts\nconst debounced = injectDebouncedValue(\n  query,\n  '',\n  () => ({ wait: wait() }),\n  undefined,\n)\n```\n\nThe fourth argument distinguishes this form from `injectDebouncedValue(query, optionsFactory, selector)`. Object options still support the existing three-argument form with an initial value.\n\n## Event handlers\n\nUse `injectDebouncer`, `injectThrottler`, or `injectRateLimiter` and call `maybeExecute()` from the event handler. For batching, use `injectBatcher` and call `addItem()`. Async utilities use the same method names.\n\nStore the utility instance in a field when options read required inputs. Accessing a method during field initialization can initialize the utility before Angular binds those inputs.\n\nIn a component:\n\n```ts\nreadonly wait = input.required<number>()\nreadonly debouncer = injectDebouncer(saveDraft, () => ({ wait: this.wait() }))\n\nsave(draft: string) {\n  this.debouncer.maybeExecute(draft)\n}\n```\n"}
